## The `HolRefute` Library

\label{sec:HolRefute}
\index{HolRefute|(}
\index{counterexamples|see {HolRefute}}
\index{model finding|see {HolRefute}}

>>__ val refute_initial_grammars =
      (Parse.type_grammar(), Parse.term_grammar());
>>__ load "Refute"; open Refute;

`HolRefute` searches for counterexamples to HOL goals by exhaustive
and random testing, symbolic narrowing, and a Kodkod-based finite model
finder, in the style of Isabelle's Quickcheck and Nitpick.  It finds
mistakes; it proves nothing.

The library is built with Poly/ML only.  A theory script uses it by
naming `Refute` under `Libs`.  The model finder needs the external
Kodkodi component (see "Installing Kodkodi" below); the other backends
are native.  Refute leaves no type, constant, theorem or binding in the
user's theory.  The `refute` theory contains the shared model-finder
encodings and preprocessing lemmas, including fraction arithmetic over
`frac`; it does not depend on `rat` or `real`.  The optional `refuteRat`
theory contains the conversion from these fractions to rationals.

### The diagnostic tactics

| Tactic | Backends |
|---|---|
| `REFUTE_TAC` | Those the configuration selects (default: all). |
| `QUICKCHECK_TAC` | `exhaustive`, `random` and `narrowing`. |
| `NARROWING_TAC` | `narrowing`. |
| `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` | `kodkod`, the model finder. |

Each tactic prints a report and then behaves as `ALL_TAC`, so it can
precede the real proof:

```sml
Theorem tail_is_shorter:
  xs <> [] ==> LENGTH (TL (xs : num list)) < LENGTH xs
Proof
  REFUTE_TAC >>
  Cases_on `xs` >> simp []
QED
```

The search looks for values of the goal's free variables that make
every assumption true and the conclusion false.  The sessions below
run backends sequentially on the `Compute` substrate, so each report
names a predictable backend.

```repl
>> set_config
     (default_config
        |> upd_sequential true
        |> upd_substrate Compute
        |> upd_size 4);

>> g `(n : num) < 3`;

>> e REFUTE_TAC;
```

### Reading a report

A counterexample report names the backend and substrate, the bound
reached, the time taken, and, for the testing backends, how many
candidates were generated, satisfied the assumptions, and had their
conclusion evaluated.  Then come the variable bindings and either
`Certified:` with a theorem refuting the goal or
`Certification: uncertified`.

When the search covers every relevant value, the result is
`NoCounterexample`:

```repl
>>__ drop();
>> g `!p q : bool. p /\ q ==> p \/ q`;

>> e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH [upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])]);
```

Otherwise the result is `Unknown`, with each backend's reasons.  At
trace level 2 the `"Refute"` trace also shows backend and substrate
choices, the schedule, and each backend's counts:

```repl
>>__ drop();
>> set_trace "Refute" 2;

>> g `!n : num. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3`;

>> e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH [upd_search QuickcheckBackends, upd_size 3]);
>>__ set_trace "Refute" 1;
```

Counts accumulate over sizes: `exhaustive` tried 2, 3 and 4 values at
sizes 1 to 3, and `random` 100 per size.  Narrowing declined the goal,
so its counts are zero.  Trace level 3 adds generated SML and plans,
level 4 a per-candidate trace on `Compute`, and level 0 silences
reports; `upd_quiet true` silences a single call.

### Configuring a search

The tactics read the stored configuration from the context they are
applied in; `set_config` replaces it and `show_config ()` prints it.
`REFUTE_TAC_WITH` applies updates to it for one call, from left to
right:

```sml
##eval REFUTE_TAC_WITH
         [upd_search (Only [Narrowing]), upd_size 7, upd_timeout 5.0]
```

`REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC` ignores the stored configuration, for reproducible
tools and tests:

```sml
##eval REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC
         (default_config |> upd_search (Only [Narrowing]) |> upd_size 7)
```

`upd_expect` makes a tactic fail unless the outcome matches one of
`ExpectNone` (that is, `NoCounterexample`), `ExpectUnknown`,
`ExpectCex`, `ExpectModel`, `ExpectNoModel`, `ExpectGenuine`,
`ExpectQuasiGenuine` or `ExpectPotential`.  All fields are listed
under "Configuration reference" below.

### Backends

| Weight | Backend | Search |
|---:|---|---|
| 20 | `exhaustive` | Enumerates values up to a size. |
| 30 | `random` | Draws seeded random values. |
| 40 | `narrowing` | Refines partially known values symbolically. |
| 50 | `kodkod` | Finds finite models with Kodkodi. |

`upd_search` takes `AllBackends`, `QuickcheckBackends` (the first
three) or `Only choices`, a nonempty list of `Exhaustive`, `Random`,
`Narrowing`, `ModelFinder` and `RegisteredBackend name`.  By default
backends race; `upd_sequential true` runs them in weight order.  A
search stops at the first result as certain as the best any selected
backend could give, and one `timeout` covers the whole call.

`exhaustive` and `random` run a compiled test plan on a substrate:
`NativeSML` compiles the goal's executable fragment to SML, and
`Compute` interprets it with `computeLib`.  The default `Auto` tries
`NativeSML`, then `Compute`.  Both draw from the same random stream,
so a seed reproduces the same candidates on either.

`narrowing` deepens from 0 up to `size`, or until the deadline in the
default iterative mode.  With `allow_existentials` it also handles
prenex existentials; with `finite_functions` it represents function
inputs as finite update chains.  Unrefined parts of a value print as
`_`.

`kodkod` encodes the goal as a bounded relational problem, covering
datatypes and codatatypes, functions, sets, inductive and coinductive
predicates, typedefs, quotients, integers, words, characters, finite
maps, and, with the optional libraries, `rat` and `real`.  Its report
gives the cardinalities tried.
This session is not generated with the manual, since building the
manual does not require Kodkodi:

```repl
> g `(x : 'a) = y`;
val it =
   Proof manager status: 1 proof.
   1. Incomplete goalstack:
        Initial goal:
        x = y

> e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH
       [upd_search (Only [ModelFinder]), upd_card [(NONE, [2])]]);
OK..
<<HOL message: Refute found a counterexample (backend: kodkod, substrate: kodkod, 1.622s):
Scope: card α = 2
  x = a2
  y = a1
Certified: ⊢ ¬∀x y. x = y
>>>
1 subgoal:
val it =

   x = y
```

With `upd_falsify false`, `kodkod` searches for a model of the goal
instead, reporting `Model` or `NoModel`.  A word of width `w` is
encoded exactly as `2^w` atoms, up to 1024 (width 10); `:char` as 256
atoms.  Word constants other than literals, `n2w`, `w2n`, arithmetic,
bitwise operations, shifts and comparisons are refused, as is a word
or character goal with binary integers forced on.

### What testing can reach

The testing backends need executable goals and a generator for each
variable's type.  With `Refute_Rat` or `Refute_Real`, the corresponding
`:rat` or `:real` type has a generator, and closed
literals are decided by `EVAL`, so a closed goal can get
`NoCounterexample`; a goal quantified over them cannot.  Finite maps
have a never-exhaustive generator on `Compute` only.  `kodkod` needs
neither, and can exhaust a finite-map goal whose key type fits its
scope.

Bounded quantifiers such as `!n. n < e ==> P n`, `!x. MEM x xs ==> P x`
and `!n. lo <= n /\ n < hi ==> P n`, and their existential duals, are
made executable.  With `smart_quantifier`, premises are tested as soon
as their variables are generated, and equations can bind variables.
With `smart_generators`, a first-order inductive premise can generate
its variables directly in exhaustive testing, and a negated
non-recursive one can become an exact test.  Here the `ALL_DISTINCT`
premise generates distinct lists, which plain generate-and-test misses
at the same bound:

```repl
>>__ drop();
>> g `ALL_DISTINCT (xs : bool list) ==> LENGTH xs < 1`;

>> e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH
        [upd_search (Only [Exhaustive]), upd_size 1, upd_depth 8]);

>> e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH
        [upd_search (Only [Exhaustive]), upd_size 1, upd_depth 8,
         upd_smart_generators false]);
```

`depth` bounds each such generator, so exhausting one is inconclusive.

### Certification and certainty

Substrates, generated SML and Kodkodi are untrusted.  With the default
`certify = true`, each testing counterexample is replayed with
`computeLib`: a replay proving the instance true discards it, and one
proving it false yields the `Certified:` theorem.  No oracle tag is
ever added.

A counterexample's `certainty` is independent of its certificate.
`Genuine` means the backend's conditions for a real counterexample
hold; `QuasiGenuine` and `Potential` carry the reasons they do not.
For `kodkod`, as for Nitpick, certainty follows the encoding: exact
gives `Genuine`, approximate `QuasiGenuine`, unsound `Potential`.  A
client needing a theorem must check `cert`.  With `certify = false`
nothing is replayed:

```repl
>>__ drop();
>> g `(n : num) < 3`;

>> e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH [upd_certify false]);
>>__ drop();
```

### Finding unused assumptions

`find_unused_assms cfgopt thy` checks which premises of a theory's
theorems can be dropped with `NoCounterexample`; `check_unused_assms`
checks one theorem, and `print_unused_assms` prints a report.  Premise
indexes start at 0:

```repl
>> val th = prove (``!p q. p ==> q ==> q``, simp []);

>> check_unused_assms NONE ("demo", th);
```

### Configuration reference

A `config` is built from `default_config` with updaters, as above.
The reference entry `Refute.config` gives the validation rules.

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_timeout` | `10.0` | Seconds for the whole call. |
| `upd_search` | `AllBackends` | Backend selection. |
| `upd_sequential` | `false` | Run backends in weight order instead of racing. |
| `upd_genuine_only` | `false` | Skip potential results and keep searching for genuine ones. |
| `upd_abort_potential` | `false` | Stop at the first result of any certainty. |
| `upd_quiet` | `false` | Print nothing. |
| `upd_no_assms` | `false` | Ignore the goal's assumptions. |
| `upd_evals` | `[]` | Terms whose values to report. |
| `upd_expect` | `NoExpectation` | Outcome the call must have. |
| `upd_max_counterexamples` | `1` | Testing counterexamples requested. |
| `upd_tag` | `""` | Text appended to the report. |
| `upd_widths` | `[1,2,3,4]` | Widths tried for a word-width type variable (1 to 32). |

#### Testing fields

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_iterative_size` | `10` | Start at this size and deepen until the deadline. |
| `upd_size` | | Fix the maximum size and narrowing depth instead. |
| `upd_iterations` | `100` | Random draws per size. |
| `upd_depth` | `10` | Bound for each smart generator. |
| `upd_finite_types` | `true` | Instantiate type variables with small finite types. |
| `upd_finite_type_size` | `3` | Number of finite types tried. |
| `upd_default_type` | `[``:num``]` | Instances when `finite_types` is false. |
| `upd_instantiate` | `[]` | Instances for particular type variables. |
| `upd_substrate` | `Auto` | `Auto`, `NativeSML` or `Compute`. |
| `upd_allow_existentials` | `true` | Narrow prenex existentials. |
| `upd_finite_functions` | `true` | Narrow function inputs as finite update chains. |
| `upd_certify` | `true` | Replay counterexamples in HOL. |
| `upd_smart_quantifier` | `true` | Test premises as early as possible. |
| `upd_smart_generators` | `true` | Generate from inductive premises. |
| `upd_optimise_equality` | `true` | Bind variables from equations. |
| `upd_reorder_premises` | `true` | Reorder premises for generation. |
| `upd_use_subtype` | `false` | Test a typedef through its representation type. |
| `upd_allow_function_inversion` | `false` | Generate from `f ... = res` premises. |
| `upd_seed` | `NONE` | Random seed; `NONE` continues the session's stream. |

`upd_instantiate` rows map `SOME 'a` to a ground type for `'a`, and
`NONE` to one for every other non-width type variable.  Instances are
proxies: a goal still polymorphic never gets `NoCounterexample` from
them.  `upd_use_subtype true` tests a variable `x` of typedef type `t`
with no generator as `abs r`, for representation values `r` satisfying
the typedef's predicate; its counterexamples are genuine but
uncertified.

#### Model-finder fields

Row-valued fields take `(NONE, v)` as the fallback and `(SOME k, v)`
for key `k`; a Boolean value of `NONE` lets Refute choose.

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_iterative_card` | `[1,...,10]` | Start with these cardinalities, by type, and grow them until the deadline. |
| `upd_card` | | Fix the cardinalities instead. |
| `upd_max` | `[~1]` | Constructor multiplicities; `~1` is automatic. |
| `upd_mono` | `NONE` | Monotonicity, by type. |
| `upd_wf` | `NONE` | Well-foundedness, by constant. |
| `upd_sat_solver` | `"smart"` | SAT solver. |
| `upd_batch_size` | `50` | Problems per solver call. |
| `upd_falsify` | `true` | Search for a counterexample, not a model. |
| `upd_user_axioms` | `NONE` | Include user axioms. |
| `upd_destroy_constrs` | `true` | Simplify constructor applications. |
| `upd_total_consts` | `NONE` | Encode constants as total. |
| `upd_peephole_optim` | `true` | Optimize the relational problem. |
| `upd_datatype_sym_break` | `5` | Datatype symmetry breaking. |
| `upd_kodkod_sym_break` | `15` | Kodkod symmetry breaking. |
| `upd_max_potential` | `1` | Potential models requested. |
| `upd_max_genuine` | `1` | Genuine models requested. |
| `upd_atoms` | `[]` | Atom names, by type. |
| `upd_format` | `[1]` | Argument tupling for display, by constant. |
| `upd_show_types` | `false` | Show type universes. |
| `upd_show_skolems` | `true` | Show Skolem values. |
| `upd_show_consts` | `false` | Show constant tables. |
| `upd_debug` | `false` | Debug output, one problem per batch. |
| `upd_overlord` | `false` | Keep the solver's files. |
| `upd_max_threads` | `0` | Kodkodi threads; `0` leaves it to Kodkodi. |
| `upd_tac_timeout` | `0.5` | Seconds for internal proofs. |
| `upd_specialize` | `true` | Specialize constants at fixed arguments. |
| `upd_box` | `NONE` | Box function and pair types, by type. |
| `upd_binary_ints` | `NONE` | Encode integers in binary. |
| `upd_bits` | `[1,...,10]` | Binary integer widths (1 to 31). |
| `upd_star_linear_preds` | `true` | Encode linear inductive predicates exactly. |
| `upd_iter` | `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]` | Unrolling depths of inductive predicates. |
| `upd_bisim_depth` | `[9]` | Codatatype bisimulation depths; `~1` disables. |
| `upd_finitize` | `NONE` | Finitize infinite types, by type. |
| `upd_whack` | `[]` | Constants whose definitions are ignored. |
| `upd_need` | `NONE` | Terms that must have values in a model. |
| `upd_merge_type_vars` | `false` | Give all type variables one cardinality. |

### The SML interface

```sml
datatype outcome =
    Counterexample of counterexample list
  | NoCounterexample
  | Model of counterexample list
  | NoModel
  | Unknown of string list
```

A `counterexample` records its `backend`, `substrate`, `certainty`,
`bindings`, `evals`, `cert`, `scope`, `model` and `stats`.

| Function | Searches |
|---|---|
| `refute cfg tm` | `tm` with `cfg`. |
| `refute_goal cfg g` | goal `g` with `cfg`. |
| `refute_with updates tm` | `tm` with `current_config updates`. |
| `quickcheck tm`, `model_refute tm` | `tm` as `QUICKCHECK_TAC`, `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC`. |
| `try_refute cfg g` | `g` silently; the finding backend, if any. |

```repl
>> Option.map #1
     (try_refute
        (default_config
           |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
           |> upd_size 4)
        ([], ``(n : num) < 3``));
```

### Extending Refute

These registrations last for the session; each reference entry gives
its conditions.

* `register_generator` and `abstract_generator` add test-data
  generators.
* `register_codatatype`, `register_typedef` and `register_quotient`
  describe types to the model finder; it finds typedefs and quotients
  with stored defining theorems by itself.
* `register_term_postprocessor` changes how reported values print.

Three theorem attributes give the model finder equations that persist
with the theory.  Each conjunct of the theorem, under its universal
quantifiers, must be an equation `c t1 ... tn = u` for a constant
`c`; `refute_simp` and `refute_psimp` also accept premises and the
forms `c t1 ... tn` and `~(c t1 ... tn)`.

| Attribute | Use of the conjuncts for `c` |
|---|---|
| `refute_simp` | Replace the equations of `c`'s definition. |
| `refute_psimp` | Define `c` if it has no equations from its definition or `refute_simp`. |
| `refute_unfold` | Unfold `c` during preprocessing. |

### Installing Kodkodi

`kodkod` needs Unix, Java, `setsid`, and Kodkodi 1.5.7 from the
Isabelle components at <https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/>.  Set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to the unpacked `kodkodi-1.5.7` directory.  Java is
taken from `HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, `JAVA_HOME/bin/java` or `PATH`;
alternatively `HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` gives a complete launcher command.

The `"smart"` solver uses SAT4J unless an external solver is found
through Nitpick's variables (`MINISAT_HOME`, `CRYPTOMINISAT_HOME`,
`ZCHAFF_HOME`, `RSAT_HOME`, `RISS3G_HOME`, `BERKMIN_HOME`,
`BERKMINALLOY_HOME`) or a JNI solver in the component's `jni`
directory; there are none for Apple Silicon.

### Limitations

* No finite search refutes `?x. P x` over an infinite type; such goals
  get at most `Potential` counterexamples.
* Model-finder reals are rational, so a goal false only at irrational
  values has no counterexample.
* A goal polymorphic after instantiation never gets `NoCounterexample`.
* The model finder sees only the equations a user wrote, not the `ARB`
  cases HOL4 adds to a non-exhaustive definition, so a goal true only
  because of them can get a spurious counterexample.
* Random testing, smart generators and narrowing are bounded, so their
  exhaustion is inconclusive.
* A datatype recursive under a function type can only be tested
  randomly.
* Smart generators do not complement recursive relations; function
  inversion covers only self-recursive functions.
* `upd_use_subtype` does not reach inside other types, such as `t list`.
* The model finder refuses word widths above 10 and unsupported word
  constants.  `WF`, `CARD`, `SUM_IMAGE`, `WFREC`, `FAPPLY` and Hilbert
  choice are encoded approximately, which can cost a goal its
  `NoCounterexample` or its `Genuine` certainty.

Worked examples of all four tactics, including case studies adapted
from Isabelle's Nitpick and Quickcheck, are in `src/HolRefute/examples`.

\index{HolRefute|)}

>>__ drop();
>>__ set_config default_config;
>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars refute_initial_grammars;
